WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
The ten risks, applied (part 2).

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:04.888
LLM06 Excessive agency.

3
00:00:05.168 --> 00:00:12.592
Giving the model more functionality, permissions, or autonomy than the task needs, so a manipulated model can do damage.

4
00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:21.491
Here, the MCP ticketing server may expose delete and reassign tools the assistant never needs, under a token with broad scope.

5
00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:34.507
Controls: expose only the tools the task requires, use narrowly scoped credentials, and require a person to approve irreversible actions (Studio 1, lesson 11, and Studio 4).

6
00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:37.624
LLM07 System prompt leakage.

7
00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:43.664
OWASP’s point is subtle: the prompt being read isn’t the real risk, because you should assume it can be.

8
00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:53.800
The risk is what teams put in prompts (credentials, internal thresholds such as refund limits, role structures) and what they rely on prompts for (authorization).

9
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.176
Controls: nothing secret in the prompt, and enforce limits in code.

10
00:00:58.456 --> 00:01:01.656
LLM08 Vector and embedding weaknesses.

11
00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:13.008
Retrieval-specific risks: one tenant’s documents retrieved for another, poisoned or hidden content in indexed documents, and inversion attacks that recover text from embeddings.

12
00:01:13.288 --> 00:01:21.523
Here, if the index ever holds customer-specific content, filter by tenant in the query itself (Studio 2), not in the prompt.

13
00:01:21.803 --> 00:01:24.149
LLM09 Misinformation.

14
00:01:24.429 --> 00:01:31.021
Confident, plausible, wrong output that users rely on, including invented policies or citations.

15
00:01:31.301 --> 00:01:37.275
Here, a hallucinated refund policy is a business and legal risk, not just a quality bug.

16
00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:44.765
Controls: grounding with verified citations, abstention, evals, and interfaces that make uncertainty visible.

17
00:01:45.045 --> 00:01:47.413
LLM10 Unbounded consumption.

18
00:01:47.693 --> 00:01:55.608
Uncontrolled resource use: denial of service, runaway cost (“denial of wallet”), and model extraction through mass querying.

19
00:01:55.888 --> 00:02:01.413
Here, an unauthenticated chat endpoint with no rate limits is an open invitation.

20
00:02:01.693 --> 00:02:10.227
Controls: input size caps, per-user rate limits and quotas, timeouts, spending alerts, and caps on agent loops (weeks 1 and 2).
